        content: "Artificial Intelligence (AI) refers to the simulation of human intelligence in machines programmed to think and learn. Unlike traditional software that follows rigid rules, AI systems can adapt their behavior based on data and experience.\n\n**Types of AI:**\n- **Narrow AI (Weak AI):** Designed for specific tasks — like Siri answering questions or Netflix recommending shows. This is what we have today.\n- **General AI (Strong AI):** Hypothetical AI that can perform any intellectual task a human can. We haven't achieved this yet.\n- **Super AI:** Theoretical AI that surpasses human intelligence in every field.\n\n**How AI Works:**\nAt its core, AI uses algorithms and statistical models to identify patterns in data. Think of it as teaching a computer to recognize patterns the way humans do — through examples rather than explicit programming.\n\n**The AI Effect:**\nInterestingly, once an AI capability becomes commonplace (like spell-check or GPS navigation), people stop calling it AI. This is known as the 'AI Effect' — we keep moving the goalpost of what counts as intelligent.",
