        id: "5-5", title: "Advanced Agent Patterns & Tool Use", duration: "16 min", type: "reading",
        content: "Beyond basic agents, advanced patterns unlock enterprise-grade capabilities.\n\n**Function Calling & Tool Use:**\nModern LLMs can decide WHEN and HOW to use tools:\n```\nUser: 'What's the weather in Mumbai and should I carry an umbrella?'\nAgent thinks: I need the weather API → calls get_weather('Mumbai') → gets data → reasons about umbrella need → responds\n```\n\n**Advanced Patterns:**\n\n**1. Agent with Guardrails:**\n- Input validation: Block harmful or out-of-scope requests\n- Output validation: Ensure responses meet quality thresholds\n- Budget limits: Cap API calls and token usage per session\n- Human-in-the-loop: Escalate high-stakes decisions\n\n**2. Self-Healing Agents:**\n- Detect when a tool call fails → retry with modified parameters\n- If Plan A fails → automatically switch to Plan B\n- Log all failures for continuous improvement\n\n**3. Agentic RAG:**\nCombine agents with RAG for powerful knowledge workers:\n- Agent decides WHAT to search for\n- Retrieves from multiple knowledge bases\n- Synthesizes across sources\n- Asks follow-up queries if initial results are insufficient\n\n**4. Code-Writing Agents:**\n- Generate code → Execute in sandbox → Check output → Fix errors → Iterate\n- Tools like Devin and OpenAI's Code Interpreter use this pattern\n\n**Production Considerations:**\n- **Observability:** Log every thought, action, and tool call\n- **Timeouts:** Set maximum execution time for agent loops\n- **Cost tracking:** Monitor per-session and per-user costs\n- **Fallback:** Always have a graceful degradation path",
        keyTakeaways: ["Function calling lets agents decide when to use which tool", "Guardrails ensure safe and bounded agent behavior", "Self-healing agents automatically recover from failures", "Production agents need observability, timeouts, and cost tracking"]
