        id: "6-1", title: "How LLMs Work", duration: "15 min", type: "reading",
        content: "Large Language Models are the foundation of modern AI. Here's what's happening under the hood.\n\n**What is an LLM?**\nA Large Language Model is a neural network trained on massive amounts of text data to predict the next word in a sequence. That simple objective — next-word prediction — gives rise to remarkably intelligent behavior.\n\n**Training Process:**\n1. **Pre-training:** Learn language patterns from billions of web pages, books, and code\n2. **Fine-tuning:** Specialize on specific tasks with curated datasets\n3. **RLHF:** Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback — humans rate outputs to improve quality\n\n**Key Concepts:**\n\n**Tokens:**\n- LLMs don't read words — they read tokens (word pieces)\n- 'Artificial Intelligence' → ['Art', 'ificial', ' Int', 'elligence'] (4 tokens)\n- Rule of thumb: 1 token ≈ 0.75 words\n- Models charge per token (input + output)\n\n**Context Window:**\n- The maximum number of tokens a model can process at once\n- GPT-4: 128K tokens (~96,000 words)\n- Claude 3: 200K tokens (~150,000 words)\n- Larger context = can handle longer documents but costs more\n\n**Parameters:**\n- Model 'size' measured in parameters (weights)\n- GPT-4: rumored ~1.8 trillion parameters\n- More parameters generally = more capable but slower and costlier",
        keyTakeaways: ["LLMs predict the next word using patterns learned from massive data", "Tokens are word pieces — roughly 1 token = 0.75 words", "Context window limits how much text a model can process at once", "Training: pre-training → fine-tuning → RLHF"]
