      {
        id: "9-5", title: "LoRA, QLoRA & Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning", duration: "15 min", type: "reading",
        content: "Full fine-tuning is expensive. Parameter-efficient methods make it accessible to everyone.\n\n**The Problem with Full Fine-Tuning:**\n- GPT-4 has ~1.8 trillion parameters — retraining all of them is impractical\n- Even a 7B model requires 28GB+ GPU memory for full fine-tuning\n- Training time: hours to days on expensive hardware\n\n**LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation):**\nInstead of updating ALL parameters, LoRA adds small trainable matrices alongside frozen model weights.\n- Trains only 0.1-1% of parameters\n- Same quality as full fine-tuning for most tasks\n- Result: A small adapter file (10-100MB) instead of a full model copy\n\n**How LoRA Works:**\n```\nOriginal weight matrix W (frozen)\n+ Low-rank matrices A × B (trainable)\n= Adapted behavior with minimal parameters\n```\n\n**QLoRA (Quantized LoRA):**\nCombines quantization (INT4) with LoRA for even lower memory:\n- Fine-tune a 65B model on a single 48GB GPU\n- Fine-tune a 7B model on a consumer GPU (16GB)\n- Minimal quality loss compared to full precision\n\n**Practical Setup:**\n```python\nfrom peft import LoraConfig, get_peft_model\n\nlora_config = LoraConfig(\n  r=16,              # Rank (lower = smaller adapter)\n  lora_alpha=32,     # Scaling factor\n  target_modules=['q_proj', 'v_proj'],\n  lora_dropout=0.05\n)\nmodel = get_peft_model(base_model, lora_config)\n# Only ~0.5% of parameters are trainable!\n```\n\n**Other PEFT Methods:**\n- **Prefix Tuning:** Prepend trainable tokens to input\n- **Prompt Tuning:** Learn soft prompts in embedding space\n- **Adapters:** Insert small trainable layers between frozen layers\n\n**When to Use What:**\n| Method | Memory | Quality | Speed | Best For |\n|--------|--------|---------|-------|----------|\n| Full Fine-tune | Very High | Best | Slow | Unlimited budget |\n| LoRA | Medium | Near-best | Fast | Most use cases |\n| QLoRA | Low | Good | Fast | Consumer hardware |\n| Prompt Tuning | Minimal | Good enough | Fastest | Simple adaptations |",
        keyTakeaways: ["LoRA trains only 0.1-1% of parameters with near-full fine-tuning quality", "QLoRA enables fine-tuning 65B models on a single GPU", "PEFT methods produce small adapter files instead of full model copies", "Choose QLoRA for consumer hardware, LoRA for most production use cases"]
      },
      {
        id: "9-6", title: "Case Study: Fine-Tuning Success Stories", duration: "14 min", type: "reading",
        content: "See how organizations use fine-tuning to build differentiated AI products.\n\n**Case Study 1: Bloomberg — BloombergGPT**\n- **Challenge:** General LLMs don't understand financial jargon, market context, or regulatory language\n- **Approach:** Trained a 50B parameter model on 363B tokens of financial data + 345B general tokens\n- **Result:** Outperformed GPT-3 on financial NLP tasks by 20-30%\n- **Key insight:** Domain-specific training data is more valuable than model size\n- **Lesson:** For specialized domains, fine-tuning on domain data creates significant competitive advantage\n\n**Case Study 2: Replit — Code Generation**\n- **Challenge:** General code models don't understand Replit's specific IDE, languages, and user patterns\n- **Approach:** Fine-tuned Code Llama on millions of Replit code sessions\n- **Result:** 40% better code completion relevance within Replit's environment\n- **Key insight:** Fine-tuning on user interaction patterns (not just code) improved UX\n- **Lesson:** Fine-tune on the full user experience, not just the core task\n\n**Case Study 3: Instacart — Shopping Assistant**\n- **Challenge:** Generic AI can't recommend products based on store inventory, dietary needs, and preferences\n- **Approach:** Fine-tuned GPT-4 on product catalog, recipes, nutritional data, and shopping patterns\n- **Result:** 'Ask Instacart' feature answers shopping questions with store-specific recommendations\n- **Key insight:** Combining fine-tuning (for behavior) with RAG (for real-time inventory) was the winning formula\n- **Lesson:** Fine-tuning + RAG together is often better than either alone\n\n**Case Study 4: Chegg — AI Study Buddy (CheggMate)**\n- **Challenge:** AI tutor must teach, not just give answers (pedagogical approach)\n- **Approach:** Fine-tuned on Socratic teaching conversations — model asks guiding questions instead of providing answers directly\n- **Result:** Students learn concepts 2x faster compared to just getting answers\n- **Key insight:** Fine-tuning changed the MODEL'S BEHAVIOR (Socratic teaching) — something prompting alone couldn't reliably achieve\n- **Lesson:** Fine-tuning shines when you need consistent behavioral patterns, not just knowledge\n\n**Decision Checklist — Should You Fine-Tune?**\n✅ You need consistent output style/format\n✅ You have 50+ high-quality examples\n✅ Prompting alone doesn't achieve desired behavior\n✅ The use case justifies the cost\n❌ You just need access to specific knowledge (use RAG)\n❌ Your requirements change frequently\n❌ A good system prompt achieves 90%+ of what you need",
        keyTakeaways: ["BloombergGPT proves domain-specific data beats raw model size", "Replit fine-tuned on user interaction patterns, not just code", "Instacart combines fine-tuning (behavior) + RAG (real-time data) for best results", "Chegg used fine-tuning to change model behavior to Socratic teaching"]
      }
